# RV32IMA Microarchitecture – Parameterization

## Goal

The goal of this project is to make the existing RV32IMA CPU parameterized, without changing its current functionality.

The CPU currently operates with a 32-bit datapath. Instead of hard-coding "32" throughout the design, the datapath will use an "XLEN" parameter.

The initial configuration will remain:

XLEN = 32

This project is not intended to create an RV64 CPU yet. The purpose is to make the current RV32IMA implementation more flexible and to potentially make a future expansion to RV64GC easier if there is enough time later in the project.

---

## Why Parameterize the CPU?

A large part of the current CPU is specifically written around 32-bit values.

For example:

logic [31:0] pc;
logic [31:0] alu_result;
logic [31:0] rs1_data;
logic [31:0] rs2_data;


This works for RV32IMA, but makes future changes to a wider architecture more difficult.

Instead, the CPU will use:

parameter XLEN = 32;

and widths will be derived from "XLEN":

logic [XLEN-1:0] pc;
logic [XLEN-1:0] alu_result;
logic [XLEN-1:0] rs1_data;
logic [XLEN-1:0] rs2_data;

The CPU will therefore still behave exactly as an RV32IMA processor when:

XLEN = 32

---

## Important Design Choice

The instruction width will remain 32 bits in this project.

The distinction is:

XLEN = 32
Instruction width = 32

"XLEN" describes the processor's register and datapath width, while the instruction width describes the size of the instructions currently supported by the CPU.

A future RV64 implementation would therefore potentially use:

XLEN = 64
Instruction width = 32

while a future implementation supporting the RISC-V Compressed extension would require additional instruction-fetch and decode changes.

Those changes are outside the scope of this project.

---

# Future RV64GC Possibility

The purpose of this parameterization is not to implement RV64GC now.

Instead, it is an architectural preparation for the possibility of extending the processor later.

A future RV64 configuration could potentially start from:

XLEN = 64

instead of:

XLEN = 32

This could make the transition from RV32IMA toward a future RV64 implementation significantly easier than if the entire CPU were built around hard coded 32-bit signals.

